         item(format!("Salt mine. Deep in the mines under the mountain, rarely, a cluster of pink salt crystals stands on the tunnel floor; break it by hand to collect {PINK_SALT_DROP_MIN} to {PINK_SALT_DROP_MAX} lumps of Pink Salt. Hold a lump in your hand and each bat that strikes you dies instead of hurting you, using up one lump. Bats live in the mineshafts and bite for {BAT_DAMAGE}.")),
         item("Compass in the Horizen and the Skylands. The needle points to where you arrived."),
-        item(format!("The jungle. Falling off the Skylands lands you in the jungle, a stretch of the normal world {JUNGLE_DISTANCE_CHUNKS} chunks from the spawn point, four times the area it first had (about {} blocks across), thick with very tall trees. In the bare middle of it stands a big Mayan-style temple: a stepped pyramid with a broad stair up its south face and a shrine on top, with a pile of gold in it. A passage in its north face leads to a chamber at its very heart, where the Cursed Idol stands on a plinth.", JUNGLE_RADIUS * 2)),
+        item(format!("The jungle. A stretch of the normal world {JUNGLE_DISTANCE_CHUNKS} chunks from where you begin, about {} blocks across, thick with very tall trees. It has no flowers and no berry bushes, but its floor is thick with foliage and figs. In the bare middle of it stands a big Mayan-style temple: a stepped pyramid with a broad stair up its south face and a shrine on top, with a pile of gold in it. A passage in its north face leads to a chamber at its very heart, where the Cursed Idol stands on a plinth. A fall from the Skylands may land you here (see The Skylands).", JUNGLE_RADIUS * 2)),
         item("Bones. Killing a deer leaves two Bones one time in five, and Bones lie on the altar islands of the Skylands: they are all one kind. Craft 3 Bones into Musical Pipes. Hold them and right click to play a tune (see Ghosts below). By day the tune calls every quail, parrot and crow within 70 blocks: they fly to you, land near you and stand still for 3 seconds. At night it calls bats instead (see Bats)."),
-        item(format!("The Horizen. A separate dimension with none of the creatures of the normal world except bats: a cave. You arrive in a pool in a domed chamber, and it is always day when you come in. Caves of every size lie all round, joined by tunnels so that you can wander from one to the next, and one winding tunnel leads to a single, far larger cavern. Nothing can be dug, so the cave is as it was made. Small ponds lie on the floor. Every {HORIZEN_CAMP_EVERY}th cave you come into has a camp: a burning log with a tin pot of red or purple berry stew, ready to ladle out and eat (right click the pot with an empty hand). Hold a Peep Stone and the silver tower appears in the great cavern, with a ghostly trail to it along the tunnel. If night falls in the cave (a Purple Berry Stew brings it) you black out and wake in the ring of Stonehenge. The Compass points back to the arrival pool; the Bone Totem does nothing there.")),
+        item(format!("The Horizen. A separate dimension with none of the creatures of the normal world except bats: a cave. You arrive in a pool in a domed chamber, and it is always day when you come in. Caves of every size lie all round, joined by tunnels so that you can wander from one to the next, and one winding tunnel leads to a single, far larger cavern with a silver tower in it. Nothing can be dug, so the cave is as it was made. Small ponds lie on the floor. About one cave in {HORIZEN_CAMP_EVERY} has a camp, always in the same place: a burning log with a tin pot of red or purple berry stew, ready to ladle out and eat (right click the pot with a wooden bowl). Hold a Peep Stone and a ghostly trail leads along the tunnel to the tower. If night falls in the cave (a Purple Berry Stew brings it) you black out and wake in the ring of Stonehenge. The Compass points back to the arrival pool; the Bone Totem leads to the king's castle.")),
         item(format!("Bosses. On the summit of the mountain lives the Yeti ({YETI_HEALTH} health): it charges when you come within {YETI_NOTICE_DISTANCE:.0} blocks, hits for {YETI_DAMAGE}, never leaves the summit's neighbourhood and is slower than you are, but enraged below half health. In the lake lives the Loch Ness Monster ({LOCH_NESS_HEALTH} health), which swims only in its lake and strikes at you from the water with its long neck. Their health bar shows at the top of the screen. Axes, the Bronze Mallet, arrows and pebbles all hurt them. The gate of the castle on a lake's island stays shut until that lake's monster is dead.")),
-        item(format!("The Skylands. Once the Yeti is dead a cyclone stands on the summit. Stand in it for {CYCLONE_SECONDS:.0} seconds, as the screen fades to white, and it carries you to the Skylands: a sky of floating islands joined by wooden bridges, with its own day and night. Step off an island and the screen goes white, and you fall from {SKY_FALL_DROP:.0} blocks above the normal world's spawn point. There is no way home but to jump off an island and fall.")),
+        item(format!("The Skylands. Once the Yeti is dead a cyclone stands on the summit. Stand in it for {CYCLONE_SECONDS:.0} seconds, as the screen fades to white, and it carries you to the Skylands: a sky of floating islands joined by wooden bridges, with its own day and night. Step off an island and the screen goes white, and you fall from {SKY_FALL_DROP:.0} blocks up on to one of three places, chosen by chance: near the top of the mountain, on the island of the great lake, or in the jungle. There is no way home but to jump off an island and fall. (Anyone wearing the King's Crown is set down in the ruined castle instead.) Poison ivy grows in patches on the islands, except the first: it stings for {POISON_IVY_DAMAGE} every second you stand in it, and breaking it by hand gives a Poison Ivy. A Poison Ivy and {POISON_IVY_ARROWS} Arrows make {POISON_IVY_ARROWS} Poison Arrows.")),
         item("The Yeti comes back every time you leave the Skylands (by falling or by dying): the summit cyclone is gone until you have killed it again. Each lake with a tower has a Loch Ness Monster of its own, and a monster that has been killed never comes back."),
         item(format!("Fire tornadoes. By day in the Skylands a large fire tornado appears every {SKY_TORNADO_INTERVAL:.0} seconds, {SKY_TORNADO_SPAWN_DISTANCE:.0} blocks away and at your height, sweeps to where you stood when it spawned, burns you on contact and stays there for {SKY_TORNADO_LIFETIME:.0} seconds, so sometimes there are four at once, each burning for {SKY_TORNADO_DAMAGE}; night makes them all disappear, and so does killing the Phoenix, for good. In the crater of the Phoenix's volcano a much larger fire tornado whirls all day, always: step into it and it burns you for {SKY_TORNADO_DAMAGE}; it too is gone at night, and killing the Phoenix does not stop it. At night the wooden bridges between the islands break: only a short ragged stub is left at each end, so you cannot cross until day.")),
-        item(format!("Crows. A crow behaves like a quail: it takes off when you come within {FLEE_DISTANCE:.0} blocks, dropping a feather, but it always flies west if the way is clear. It leaves {CROW_FEATHER_DROP} feathers and no poultry. Follow one crow west for two chunks ({CROW_ALTAR_WEST_BLOCKS:.0} blocks, staying within {CROW_FOLLOW_DISTANCE:.0} blocks of it) and it lands on a new ancient altar, a small altar with a Loadstone on it. Take the Loadstone by hand; the altar stays. If you already have a Loadstone or a Compass, crows build no altar.")),
+        item(format!("Crows. A crow behaves like a quail: it takes off when you come within {FLEE_DISTANCE:.0} blocks, dropping a feather, and it always flies towards the nearest ancient altar within {CROW_ALTAR_SEEK_DISTANCE:.0} blocks (away from you if there is none), coming down on it when it gets there. It leaves {CROW_FEATHER_DROP} feathers and no poultry. Ancient altars are rare and stand where they always stood, far from where the world began: a small altar with a Loadstone on it. Take the Loadstone by hand; the altar stays. Follow a crow, and scare it on each time it settles, and it leads you to one.")),
         item(format!("Iron Quail. Every {QUAIL_KILLS_PER_IRON_QUAIL} quail you kill, an Iron Quail ({IRON_QUAIL_HEALTH} health) appears and hunts you for as long as you both live: she never gives up, and she flies: climbing after you, over obstacles and up to you wherever you stand. She hits for {IRON_QUAIL_DAMAGE}, and when she dies she drops {IRON_QUAIL_IRON_DROP} Iron and {IRON_QUAIL_IRON_FEATHER_DROP} Iron Feathers. If you die she is gone.")),
         item("Tin Pan. Made of 2 Tin. Hold it and break river clay: one time in twenty a Gold turns up as well, one time in five with a Rabbit Foot in your hotbar."),
         item("The Philosopher's Stone. Right click a boulder with it to turn the boulder to gold; the stone is kept. A gold boulder breaks by hand into gold."),
-        item(format!("The King's Crown and Excalibur. The crown from the Horizen tower is worn like other garments (right click). Wear it in the normal world and a large ruined castle waits ahead of you, far off beyond the land you can see. In its courtyard a sword stands in a stone. Right click it while wearing the crown to draw Excalibur, the best weapon in the game ({EXCALIBUR_DAMAGE} damage, reaches {EXCALIBUR_REACH:.1} blocks). Without the crown the sword will not move.")),
+        item(format!("The King's Crown and Excalibur. The crown from the Horizen tower is worn like other garments (right click). A large ruined castle stands far away in the normal world. In its courtyard a sword stands in a stone. Right click it while wearing the crown to draw Excalibur, the best weapon in the game ({EXCALIBUR_DAMAGE} damage, reaches {EXCALIBUR_REACH:.1} blocks). Without the crown the sword will not move. Whenever you come back to the normal world from the Skylands or the Horizen while wearing the crown, you are set down in the ruined castle, just south of the sword.")),
         item("Wearing. You wear one thing at a time. Right click a Pelt Cloak (made of a Bear Pelt), the Royal Armour, the King's Crown or a Leather Backpack to put it on, or drag it onto the Worn slot in the inventory; click the Worn slot to take it off. They all share that one slot. While you wear a Pelt Cloak, bears leave you alone unless you hit them first."),
         item(format!("Leather Backpack. Made of 4 Leather and 2 Leather Cord. Right click it to put it on. It is worn in the same place as a cloak, the armour or the crown (the Worn slot), so you can wear only one of them at a time. While it is on, the inventory has {BACKPACK_EXTRA_ROWS} more rows of slots. To take it off, or to put on something else in its place, what is in its rows has to fit in the other slots first.")),
-        item(format!("Ghosts. You cannot see or touch ghosts: their words just appear on the screen. In four places they speak. The ghost of the graveyard asks, as you walk in, for a song so that it may finally rest: play it a tune on Musical Pipes there and it thanks you, gives you a Silver Key, tells you that in three lonely towers there are others who may like to hear the music, and never speaks again. In each silver tower (the Skylands, the cave and a lake island), playing a tune brings a ghostly voice with a hint. The Skylands ghost tells you to wade through the river until you find the island, and not to leave the water until you do; the cave ghost says that things work differently at night, so try your tools at night; the ghost of the lake tower tells you to blow the horn in the stonehenge (see The Wild Hunt).")),
+        item(format!("Ghosts. You cannot see or touch ghosts: their words just appear on the screen. In four places they speak. The ghost of the graveyard asks, as you walk in, for a song so that it may finally rest: play it a tune on Musical Pipes there and it thanks you, gives you a Silver Key, tells you that in three lonely towers there are others who may like to hear the music, and never speaks again. In each silver tower (the Skylands, the cave and a lake island), playing a tune brings a ghostly voice with a hint. The Skylands ghost tells you to find the great Loch guarded by that old serpent; the cave ghost says that things work differently at night, so try your tools at night; the ghost of the lake tower tells you to blow the horn in the stonehenge (see The Wild Hunt).")),
         item(format!("Stonehenge. A ring of {STONEHENGE_STONES} tall standing stones, with lintels over pairs of them, round an altar stone, about {STONEHENGE_DISTANCE_CHUNKS} chunks from where the world began. If night falls in the cave you black out and wake in its ring. It is where the Horn of the Hunt is blown (see The Wild Hunt).")),
         item(format!("Bats. Bats do not live in the normal world. Play Musical Pipes at night and {BATS_PER_PIPES} bats come out of the dark and fly straight at you (never more than {MAX_BAT_OVERWORLD} at once): each bites for {BAT_DAMAGE} every {BAT_ATTACK_COOLDOWN:.1} seconds and dies to a single arrow or blow. They are gone at dawn. In the Horizen's cave bats live naturally (up to {MAX_BAT_CAVE} near you at a time) and attack when you come within {BAT_NOTICE_DISTANCE:.0} blocks.")),
         item(format!("The Wild Hunt. The last boss. Take the Horn of the Hunt from the top of the Skylands tower, go into the ring of Stonehenge and right click with the horn to blow it. A ghostly host rides out and hunts you, with one health bar between them. The swordsman ({HUNT_SWORDSMAN_HEALTH} health) charges you and cuts for {HUNT_SWORD_DAMAGE}; it is slower than a sprint. Two archers ({HUNT_ARCHER_HEALTH} health each) stay {HUNT_ARCHER_MIN_RANGE:.0} to {HUNT_ARCHER_MAX_RANGE:.0} blocks off, side-stepping, and each shoots an arrow every {HUNT_ARCHER_INTERVAL:.0} seconds for {HUNT_ARROW_DAMAGE}; keep a Rabbit Foot in your hotbar and they fire only half as often. Two lancers on horses ({HUNT_LANCER_HEALTH} health each) charge straight at you, faster than a sprint, and run you through for {HUNT_LANCER_DAMAGE}, then gallop away, rest for a few seconds and charge again. {HUNT_GHOST_BEARS} ghostly bears and {HUNT_GHOST_CROWS} ghostly crows come too: when you kill one of the first {HUNT_GHOST_BEARS} bears or the first {HUNT_GHOST_CROWS} crows, {HUNT_SPLIT_INTO} more of its kind appear (and those do not split). Excalibur does {EXCALIBUR_HUNT_FACTOR} times its damage to all of them. If you die they are gone, and the horn can call them again. Beat them all and you win the game; you can keep playing.")),
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@ pub fn help_lines() -> Vec<Line> {
         item(format!("Ravens. At night ravens circle you at a distance, then swoop down for {RAVEN_DAMAGE} and fly away again. They go with the dawn.")),
         item(format!("Iron Quail feathers. Each Iron Quail (three come every {QUAIL_KILLS_PER_IRON_QUAIL} quail) strikes you, and from a distance shoots iron feathers for {IRON_FEATHER_DAMAGE}. A feather that misses lies on the ground: pick it up, hold it and click to throw it like a throwing star ({IRON_FEATHER_THROW_DAMAGE} damage). It is used up only when it hits an animal. (Silver Keys come from the mines and the graveyard's ghost; if you already have one, or one lies on the ground, anything that would drop another drops a gold bar instead.)")),
         item(format!("Cave bats and the Vampire Bat. Bats now come into the Horizen's caves half as often as they did. For every {BATS_PER_VAMPIRE} bats you kill in the caves a Vampire Bat ({VAMPIRE_BAT_HEALTH} health) comes out of the dark. It has one attack: it circles you, swoops down for {VAMPIRE_BAT_DAMAGE}, flies off and comes again. It drops {VAMPIRE_BAT_GOLD_DROP} Gold. Out of its swoop it keeps to the middle of the passage it is in, away from the ceiling and the walls.")),
-        item(format!("The King's castle. In the Horizen a giant cave, {KING_CAVE_CELLS} cave-squares from where you arrive, holds the largest castle in the game: an outer wall {} blocks across with a gate on the south side, four towers and a great keep. Hold a Bone Totem in the Horizen and its trail leads through the caves to it. Play Musical Pipes inside the walls and the ghost of the king speaks: \"I am the king eternal. Give me gold and I shall send my spirits to guard you.\" A pop-up lets you give him gold: for every {GOLD_PER_SPIRIT} gold one spirit archer follows you. Each shoots {SPIRIT_SHOTS} arrows at whatever you have hit lately, then vanishes. They go with you into any other dimension. In the middle of the keep, on a dais, are the Boots of the Sky.", KING_WALL_HALF * 2 + 1)),
+        item(format!("The King's castle. In the Horizen a giant cave, {KING_CAVE_CELLS} cave-squares from where you arrive, holds the largest castle in the game: an outer wall {} blocks across with a gate on the south side and four towers, and inside it a dungeon of two floors. Hold a Bone Totem in the Horizen and its trail leads through the caves to it. Eight rooms lie one after another: the gatehouse, a hall of pillars, the armoury, the barracks and the crypt on the ground floor, then a stair up to the chapel and the library, and last the throne room. Step into each room but the last and {KING_GUARDS_PER_ROOM} hostile spirit guards come out and shoot at you; the door out of the room stays shut until they are dead, and then it opens for good. Leave a room with its guards alive and they stand down until you come back. In the throne room there are no guards. Play Musical Pipes there and the ghost of the king speaks: \"I am the king eternal. Give me gold and I shall send my spirits to guard you.\" A pop-up lets you give him gold: for every {GOLD_PER_SPIRIT} gold one spirit archer follows you. Each shoots {SPIRIT_SHOTS} arrows at whatever you have hit lately, then vanishes. They go with you into any other dimension. The king gives his spirits nowhere else. On a dais in the throne room are the Boots of the Sky.", KING_WALL_HALF * 2 + 1)),
         item(format!("Boots of the Sky. Wear them (right click, or the Worn slot; they share the one worn place) and you run {BOOTS_SPEED_FACTOR} times as fast and jump {BOOTS_JUMP_DISTANCE_FACTOR} times as far.")),
         item(format!("Parrots and Bronze Parrots. Parrots are colourful quail that live in the jungle, where only the jungle's own creatures come (no deer, quail, bears, rabbits, moose or ravens). Every {PARROTS_PER_BRONZE_PARROT} parrots you kill, {BRONZE_PARROTS_PER_SUMMON} Bronze Parrots ({BRONZE_PARROT_HEALTH} health) come for you and fight like Iron Quails; each leaves {BRONZE_PARROT_BRONZE_DROP} Bronze and {BRONZE_PARROT_IRON_FEATHER_DROP} Iron Feathers.")),
         item(format!("Cursed Idol. Taken from its plinth in the heart of the jungle temple, whose passage is boarded up until the Orangutan is dead. While it is anywhere in your hotbar you deal {IDOL_DAMAGE_FACTOR} times the damage, but nothing can heal you: berries, meat and red stew all refuse.")),
@@ -118,19 +118,19 @@ pub fn help_lines() -> Vec<Line> {
         item(format!("Crystals and the Sling. A Sling shoots a crystal (Ruby, then Sapphire, then Amethyst) before it uses a pebble, for {CRYSTAL_DAMAGE_FACTOR} times a pebble's damage. A crystal that misses lies on the ground and can be picked up again.")),
         item(format!("Mushrooms. Break one in a mushroom cave and eat it (right click): it costs you {MUSHROOM_DAMAGE} life, and for {MUSHROOM_BOOST_SECONDS:.0} seconds you run {MUSHROOM_SPEED_FACTOR} times as fast.")),
         item(format!("The Crystal Ball. {CRYSTAL_BALL_STARDUST} Stardust (the white chunks that fall from the sky on Skylands nights) make a Crystal Ball. Hold it and left click: night turns to day, or day to night, behind the usual blackout. It does nothing in the Horizen, which is always day.")),
-        item(format!("Crawfish. Small crawfish ({CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health) live in the rivers and never attack; one in five leaves a Shell Fragment. In the Horizen's river caves live big crawfish ({BIG_CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health): they leave you alone until you hurt one, then they fight back for {BIG_CRAWFISH_DAMAGE}, and always leave {BIG_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MIN} or {BIG_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MAX} fragments. Kill {BIG_CRAWFISH_KILLS_PER_GIANT} big crawfish and the Giant Crawfish ({GIANT_CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health) rises from the cave water; it leaves {GIANT_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MIN} to {GIANT_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MAX} fragments. All of them keep to the water.")),
+        item(format!("Crawfish. Small crawfish ({CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health) live in the rivers and never attack; one in five leaves a Shell Fragment. In the Horizen's river caves live big crawfish ({BIG_CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health): they leave you alone until you hurt one, then they fight back for {BIG_CRAWFISH_DAMAGE}, and always leave {BIG_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MIN} or {BIG_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MAX} fragments. Kill {BIG_CRAWFISH_KILLS_PER_GIANT} big crawfish and the Giant Crawfish ({GIANT_CRAWFISH_HEALTH} health) rises from the cave water; it leaves {GIANT_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MIN} to {GIANT_CRAWFISH_FRAGMENT_MAX} fragments. Crawfish keep to the water, except that a big or giant one that is attacking comes out of it to chase you, and goes back to the water when it gives up.")),
         item(format!("Shell Armor. {SHELL_ARMOR_FRAGMENTS} Shell Fragments make a Shell Armor. Worn (right click it), it takes {SHELL_ARMOR_REDUCTION} off every blow you suffer, but never more than half of it.")),
         item(format!("The Dragon. After {DRAGON_SWITCHES} day/night switches (by stew or Crystal Ball) a Dragon ({DRAGON_HEALTH} health), the biggest creature in the game, comes to the Skylands. It never lands. It breathes a stream of fire ({DRAGON_FLAME_DAMAGE} for each flame that reaches you) and swoops down for {DRAGON_SWOOP_DAMAGE}, throwing you {DRAGON_SWOOP_KNOCKBACK:.0} blocks. It drops {DRAGON_GOLD_DROP} gold, and killing it starts the count again.")),
         item(format!("The Unicorn. Nothing needs clicking: the first time you carry the Cursed Idol in your hotbar in the normal world, outside the jungle, a Unicorn ({UNICORN_HEALTH} health) steps out of the trees near you. It will not attack while you are in the jungle, but once you leave it, it charges at you wherever you go and never gives up. It leaves {UNICORN_GOLD_DROP} gold and a Unicorn Horn; a Horn and a Leather Cord craft a Unicorn Sword, which hits for {UNICORN_SWORD_DAMAGE} (Excalibur does {EXCALIBUR_DAMAGE}) but, unlike Excalibur, does no extra damage to the Wild Hunt. If you leave the world before killing it, it is waiting when you return.")),
         item(format!("The Great Bear. Kill a bear while you wear the Pelt Cloak and a huge, dark bear ({BEAR_BOSS_HEALTH} health, red-eyed) comes out of the trees about {BEAR_BOSS_SPAWN_DISTANCE:.0} blocks away and charges you from any distance; the cloak does not calm it. It hits for {BEAR_BOSS_DAMAGE} and, below half health, is quicker. There is only one at a time, and it hunts you until you die or kill it (it leaves {BEAR_BOSS_MEAT_DROP} Bear Meat, {BEAR_BOSS_PELT_DROP} Bear Pelts and {BEAR_BOSS_GOLD_DROP} Gold). Every time you hit it, every bear in the world, near or far, and any that appear, comes for you for {BEAR_BOSS_RAGE_SECONDS:.0} seconds (the cloak no longer calms them). Take the cloak off before you hunt bears, if you would rather not meet it.")),
         item(format!("Falling stars. On a Skylands night a star streaks down from the sky about every {FALLING_STAR_INTERVAL:.0} seconds and lands, in a flash of light, on an island you can see, never the one you are standing on. It leaves a white chunk of stardust that fades after {STARDUST_PILE_SECONDS:.0} seconds; break it before then to get Stardust.")),
-        item(format!("The jungle floor. The jungle is full of small ponds you can wade through, and its grass is darker than the forest's. Broad-leaved foliage grows on the ground there: break it by hand ({BREAK_SECONDS_GROUND_FOLIAGE:.0} second) and it drops nothing, but about one clump in {JUNGLE_FIG_ONE_IN} bears figs. Right click one to pick {FIG_PICK_MIN} to {FIG_PICK_MAX} figs (it stays, bare), or break it for {FIG_BREAK_MIN} to {FIG_BREAK_MAX}. A fig heals {FIG_HEAL}.")),
+        item(format!("The jungle floor. The jungle is full of small ponds you can wade through, and its grass is darker than the forest's. It has no flowers and no berry bushes. Broad-leaved foliage grows thick on the ground there: break it by hand ({BREAK_SECONDS_GROUND_FOLIAGE:.0} second) and it drops nothing, but about one clump in {JUNGLE_FIG_ONE_IN} bears figs. Right click one to pick {FIG_PICK_MIN} to {FIG_PICK_MAX} figs (it stays, bare), or break it for {FIG_BREAK_MIN} to {FIG_BREAK_MAX}. A fig heals {FIG_HEAL}.")),
         item(format!("Fairies. Small winged fairies flit about the Skylands' islands ({FAIRY_HEALTH} health) and now and then plant a flower. They never hurt you, but hit one and every fairy turns on you: they push you ({FAIRY_KNOCKBACK:.0} blocks) from the side that sends you over the nearest edge. Left alone, a fairy will trade: right click one holding Ash and you get a Vial of Moonlight. The general store's ghost will remind you if you carry ash. Drinking a vial (right click) gives one extra heart for {MOONLIGHT_SECONDS:.0} seconds, up to {MOONLIGHT_MAX_BONUS} extra; each vial drunk starts every running timer over.")),
         item(format!("Mountain goats. White goats ({GOAT_HEALTH} health) graze the Skylands' islands and leave you alone until you hit one; then it charges, butting for {GOAT_DAMAGE} and throwing you {GOAT_KNOCKBACK:.0} blocks. It drops {GOAT_HORN_DROP} Goat Horn, and a horn makes {IVORY_KNIVES_PER_HORN} Ivory Knives. Hold one and click to throw it ({IVORY_KNIFE_DAMAGE} damage); it is never used up: it falls where it hits, and you can pick it up again.")),
         item(format!("Caves of the Horizen. The caves are of five kinds: plain; mossy, with green pools ringed with moss; river caves, with a stream winding across the floor (shallow enough to wade); crystal caves, with clusters of ruby (red), sapphire (blue) and amethyst (purple) that you break by hand for {CRYSTAL_DROP_MIN} to {CRYSTAL_DROP_MAX} crystals each; and mushroom caves, where mushrooms grow that you pick by hand. You always arrive in the same pool, and the screen goes black before you are moved.")),
         item(format!(
